 back  suddenly we're on Dave Dave is talking  back and forth back and forth and you  know me the in stride  who's being filmed right now I'm not  even in the picture it doesn't matter  what I what I'm saying or thinking or  doing you know that maybe then you know  where the camera person starts looking  for clothes reaction shots of other  students you know does everyone agree  with you or does everyone agree with  Dave and think you're wrong you know and  and as me and as you make that choice is  a camera person it's going to impact who  you choose to film so you know we went  we touched on the idea of fairness and  accuracy in the documentary film you  know what you choose to frame and how  you choose to frame it has a huge huge  impact on the story that you tell so you  can walk into that situation I just  described and film it with an agenda  like you know we want to make Sabrina  look like the smartest person in the  room so then you film it in a certain  way or you know we want to just we want  to show how you know how completely  isolated she is so it in a different way  or you could be very sensitive and open  to the the situation and adjust and try  to be making accurate or fair  representation of what's actually  happened and believe me that's that both  are common in in documentary film I'm  going in with an agenda is extremely  common and that's not the same as you  know lying quote/unquote manipulating  although there is an element of  manipulation to it but but generally we  do go in with preconceived notions and  it impacts how we how we frame things on  how we shoot things so anyway that's  kind of the overall thing we're not  going to talk about a lot of film  grammar right now because I could go on  for the rest of the time and I'd rather  get on to the actual camerawork but I am  going to I am going to just raise a  couple of things to think about since  you very soon have cameras in your hands  and you know just just basic kind of  kinds of things to think about as you  choose the shots that you that you're  going to  I'm gonna film first of all there's a  real tendency when you get a camera the  further you know for the first time and  I mean I'm you I'm sure everyone at this  point is filmed something you know with  a video camera there's a real tendency  to want to capture everything right so  we're we're in this room we're filming  and you sort of think like oh I'm gonna  get everything and you start doing this  which is basically moving continually  because that's how we our eyes work  right you know I'm I'm just looking  around I see everything so you just  assume the camera works that way too but  what you end up doing is capturing  nothing because with the camera you have  to you have to sort of fake the way your  eye works and the way your eye works is  I'm really I'm doing this I'm seeing  everything but I'm also continuously you  know my brain is continuously picking  out things and making close-ups out of  the stuff that I see making different  angles so when I was talking about you  suddenly there was a close-up of you  even though I saw everything else to  when I talked about Dave suddenly there  was a close-up of day in my head right  so the camera has to do that and you  have to make it do that which means that  you know what they call hosing down the  room which is this kind of continual  sort of well I don't want to you know I  don't want to miss anything so I'm just  gonna keep doing this it just ends up  with with mush and and you're not  telling a story and you're not really  getting any shots that you're able to  edit so you know you want to be  deliberate about what you're shooting  and you want to think about what you're  shooting you know what's the story am i  telling and what what images do I need  to tell that story you know do I need to  focus on on you do I need to focus on  Dave do I need to shoot a wide shot that  establishes the geography of the room so  that I know where people are sitting  second thing is very basic thing hold  your shots you know there's also a  tendency that goes along with this this  hosing down the room to never really  hold on anything so I'm filming Sabrina  but then suddenly I want to go over here  and then I want to go back and then  end up with nothing again so you you  know you want to want to make a choice  and then stick with it for long enough  that you actually get something so if  I'm shooting a close-up of Chris  listening to me  skeptically I want to hold on for an  absurd number of seconds and then go  back to the next thing that I want to  shoot and it can be hard sometimes  because you know suddenly something  really interesting is happening over  there and you want to run over and get  it and you need to be responsive to that  and obviously pay attention to it but if  you get sort of too caught up in the  moment you end up with nothing and and  this is something that is a real  struggle even for professional camera  people sometimes because they they have  all this sort of information bombarding  them about what they want to do and they  some of them especially people who come  from still photography maybe don't hold  the shots long enough you know there's  kind of a rule of you know like the nice  long slow count to ten  before you then pan away to something  else it could be a count to five the  action is a little faster but just  remember you know frame something up  hold the shot and then move on and then  you know the idea also when you're  moving the camera is is think about why  you're moving the camera why would you  move it why would you move the camera  rather than just to hose down the room  any basic ideas  well I know if I'm moving if I if I have  a camera  why would I actually start doing this  why would I actually move well compared  to compared to just moving like this  randomly or not moving at all  you know what would motivate me to move  the camera right yep exactly so  following an action and it might be an  actually a physical action I mean if I'm  if I'm profiling Chris and suddenly she  gets up and goes over and you know to  like you know really getting days face  to really argue with them I you know I'd  want to follow that right but if they're  just talking  even cordially I don't know why I always  bring you into conflict Dave yeah you  just draw you know you used try it out  of it but you know even if they're just  they're speaking as you say I would be  panning back and forth and at some point  I might come over here and film Dave and  start filming from his point of view so  that's the you know that's the situation  you try to we want to try to make your  moves motivated by something not just by  kind of random closing down of the room  so that's again just a really basic  concept and all of this is you know it's  going to make a lot more sense when you  start shooting and it's really going to  make sense when you start editing  because editing is the part where you  really learn how to shoot I think all  people who shoot should edit even if  they don't want to be editors because  that's when you really learned what's  working and what's not working in your  shooting a couple of other quick tips  remember that although film is a  two-dimensional art form and your images  are all 2d you're representing a 3d  world and that means that you want to  think about ways to create depth in your  frame which means essentially different  planes of action  so again if I'm filming Chris and Dave  if I come around like this and I shoot  Chris's shoulder in the foreground and  Dave in the Far background that's kind  of a nice deep shot because we're we got  a sense of the room we get a sense that  there's depth that there's kind of a  dimensionality to to the conversation  and then I can go over and I can do the  same this way you know I also might  still shoot close-ups and edited it all  together but you know what would be kind  of weird an